BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
DTSTART:20261106T140000Z
DTEND:20261106T211500Z
LOCATION:LOWELL E. BAIER HALL/IU MAURER SCHOOL OF LAW
SUMMARY:ILJ Symposium: Evolving Frontiers in Criminal Law: Pregnancy\, Fa
 mily\, and Identity
DESCRIPTION:The Symposium originated from the book\, Pink Crime: Fighting
  Against the Criminalization of Motherhood\, Pregnancy\, and Queer Identi
 ty\, authored by Professor Valena Beety\, the Robert H. McKinney Professo
 r of Law at Indiana University Maurer School of Law and a founding board 
 member of the Indiana Innocence Project. Pink Crime focuses on the ways t
 hat criminal law is strategically used to limit the bodily autonomy of wo
 men and queer people\, combined with a discussion of how the system as a 
 whole targets the most vulnerable populations while allowing bias\, stigm
 a\, and unreliable evidence to influence prosecution decisions\, especial
 ly in situations where no crime occurred at all. As the book explores\, t
 he modern American legal system disproportionately punishes and often cri
 minalizes identity\, specifically in situations where pregnancy\, motherh
 ood\, or queer identity are present.  This Symposium worked to expand upo
 n Professor Beety's work in Pink Crime by bringing in additional perspect
 ives to illustrate how existing and developing legal mechanisms strip awa
 y basic rights and lay the groundwork for more oppressive measures in the
  future. The Symposium convened a group of nationally-respected experts i
 n wrongful convictions\, criminal law\, reproductive justice\, family law
 \, family policing\, faulty forensics\, gender law\, and health law\, amo
 ng others. Their combined collection of work addressed emerging trends in
  prosecution\, evidence\, and punishment related to the topic.  This area
  of law is evolving too quickly for large portions of the legal academy t
 o keep up. Importantly\, this Symposium fills a gap in the existing legal
  scholarship. Discussions on pregnancy crime and gender affirming care ar
 e reaching the highest levels of state\, national\, and international gov
 ernance\, and informed scholarship is paramount as decisions continue to 
 be made that affect massive amounts of individuals across the country\, a
 nd even across the globe. Therefore\, this Symposium aims to analyze the 
 legal\, social\, and political dimensions of identity criminalization and
  provide timely theory\, analysis\, and practical solutions for professio
 nals to draw on as we move forward.ILJ associates are required to attend 
 at least two panels. Attendance will be taken.Schedule&#160\;9:00 – 10:
 00 AM Panel 1: The Prosecution of Pregnancy Behaviors and Outcomes10:15 �
 �� 11:15 AM Panel 2: Criminal Law After Dobbs11:30 AM – 12:30 PM Panel 
 3: Family Regulation Systems and Criminal Law2:00 – 3:00 PM Panel 4: Cr
 iminalizing Queer Life and Family3:15 – 4:15 PM Panel 5: Systemic Failu
 res along the Path to Wrongful Convictions  Each of these five lectures h
 as been approved for 1 hour of CLE credit by the Indiana Office of Admiss
 ions and Continuing Education.&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The Symposium originated from the book\
 , <em>Pink Crime: Fighting Against the Criminalization of Motherhood\, Pr
 egnancy\, and Queer Identity</em>\, authored by Professor Valena Beety\, 
 the Robert H. McKinney Professor of Law at Indiana University Maurer Scho
 ol of Law and a founding board member of the Indiana Innocence Project. <
 em>Pink Crime</em> focuses on the ways that criminal law is strategically
  used to limit the bodily autonomy of women and queer people\, combined w
 ith a discussion of how the system as a whole targets the most vulnerable
  populations while allowing bias\, stigma\, and unreliable evidence to in
 fluence prosecution decisions\, especially in situations where no crime o
 ccurred at all. As the book explores\, the modern American legal system d
 isproportionately punishes and often criminalizes identity\, specifically
  in situations where pregnancy\, motherhood\, or queer identity are prese
 nt.</p><p>  This Symposium worked to expand upon Professor Beety's work i
 n <em>Pink Crime</em> by bringing in additional perspectives to illustrat
 e how existing and developing legal mechanisms strip away basic rights an
 d lay the groundwork for more oppressive measures in the future. The Symp
 osium convened a group of nationally-respected experts in wrongful convic
 tions\, criminal law\, reproductive justice\, family law\, family policin
 g\, faulty forensics\, gender law\, and health law\, among others. Their 
 combined collection of work addressed emerging trends in prosecution\, ev
 idence\, and punishment related to the topic.</p><p>  This area of law is
  evolving too quickly for large portions of the legal academy to keep up.
  Importantly\, this Symposium fills a gap in the existing legal scholarsh
 ip. Discussions on pregnancy crime and gender affirming care are reaching
  the highest levels of state\, national\, and international governance\, 
 and informed scholarship is paramount as decisions continue to be made th
 at affect massive amounts of individuals across the country\, and even ac
 ross the globe. Therefore\, this Symposium aims to analyze the legal\, so
 cial\, and political dimensions of identity criminalization and provide t
 imely theory\, analysis\, and practical solutions for professionals to dr
 aw on as we move forward.</p><p><span style="text-decoration: underline\;
 ">ILJ associates are required to attend at least two panels. Attendance w
 ill be taken.</span></p><p><strong>Schedule&#160\;</strong></p><p><strong
 >9:00 – 10:00 AM Panel 1: <span style="text-decoration: underline\;">Th
 e Prosecution of Pregnancy Behaviors and Outcomes</span></strong></p><p><
 strong>10:15 – 11:15 AM Panel 2: <span style="text-decoration: underlin
 e\;">Criminal Law After Dobbs</span></strong></p><p><strong>11:30 AM – 
 12:30 PM Panel 3: <span style="text-decoration: underline\;">Family Regul
 ation Systems and Criminal Law</span></strong></p><p><strong>2:00 – 3:0
 0 PM Panel 4: <span style="text-decoration: underline\;">Criminalizing Qu
 eer Life and Family</span></strong></p><p><strong>3:15 – 4:15 PM Panel 
 5: <span style="text-decoration: underline\;">Systemic Failures along the
  Path to Wrongful Convictions</span></strong></p><p>  Each of these five 
 lectures has been approved for 1 hour of CLE credit by the Indiana Office
  of Admissions and Continuing Education.&#160\;</p>
UID:20261106T140000Z-2435593@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260930T083050Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2435593-ilj-symposium-evolving-fro
 ntiers-in-criminal-law
LAST-MODIFIED:20261007T205813Z
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
